2.Primary Key - Its a column and used to uniquely indentify the row in the table.Primary key is unique and not null. Example consider an employee table where it has, employeeid, fname, lname, districtid. Here we can choose employeeid as primary key because, we cannot have a duplicate record for employeeid and cant be null, so such thing should be primary key and with primary key we can uniquely identify the data in table. for eg i need to retrieve employee uid 5 info, i can get it by using primary key.
3.Foreign Key - Its is used to join two table. Foreign key in one parent table will be the primary key in the child table. Eg from above employee table district id is foreign key and in district table we have district id as primary key. Its help us to connect tow table. For example i need to get employeee district info, i can get it by using foreign key.In primary key table itself i can write district info, but employee may be come from same district. Coniser we have 1K employee and all are from 3 districts, i need to duplicate the district name in primary table but instead that i can have district table and i have that district infor and other related info in that table and i can referance that in primary table using foreign key , it will reduce time and memory as well.We can have multiple foreign key in primary table.
If we run select startement we can get the details which is primary key, but to get the what are all the foreign key in table we need to go to pgadmin and select the table then constraints it will show foreign key info. Else click on same place click on FK and properties, it will show its is referance from which table
4. Constraints - It is used to apply the conditions on both columns and table ie how the column value to be inseted or how tables to be created.
Common column constraints:
NOT NULL - We cant enter the null value in that column if we have not null constraint
UNIQUE - All the value entered in that column must be unique
PRIMARY_KEY - Should be the primary key for that table and adher primary key rule
FOREIGN_KEY - Its is used to join two table.Should  have primary key in another table
CHECK - If it mach the condition like <20 then only allow us to enter that value
EXCLUSION -
Similarly we have table level constraint as well , it will apply for entire table.
5. CREATE TABLE - Used to create a table
Syntax:
CREATE TABLE table_name(
col_name1 TYPE col_constraint1 col_constraint2,
col_name2 TYPE col_constraint1 col_constraint2 col_constraint2
table_constraint table_constraint
)INHERITS external_table_name;
Eg: I created 3 table
Table 1:
CREATE TABLE account(
user_id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
username VARCHAR(200) UNIQUE NOT NULL,
password VARCHAR(200) NOT NULL,
emailid VARCHAR(200) UNIQUE NOT NULL,
created_on TIMESTAMP NOT NULL,
last_login TIMESTAMP
);
Table 2:
CREATE TABLE job(
jobid SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
job_desc VARCHAR(200) NOT NULL
);
Table 3:
CREATE TABLE account_job(
user_id INTEGER REFERENCES account(user_id),
jobid INTEGER REFERENCES job(jobid),
hired_on TIMESTAMP NOT NULL
);
Here what i have done is i created 3 table and table 1 i set user_id as primary key and set type as serial, so what this serial will do is its automatically inbcrement the value we no need to manually insert. Always for PK we use serial. for user name we have set constraing as should not be null and unique like the same for table 2 as well.For table 3 what i did is i created a table and set 2 FK here user_id and jobid here on Type i given as integrer the reason is when we create a fk, it should be the same datatype of pk but bot serial. here our case its integrer.in table 3 i created 2 fk along with table unique column and we did not mention pk for table 3

