7. UPDATE - Updating the existing column value into table
Syntax:
UPDATE table_name
SET col_name1 = value1,
col_name2 = value2, ....
WHERE condition;
Example
SELECT * FROM account;
SELECT * FROM job;
SELECT * FROM account_job;
Updating the column value
UPDATE account
SET last_login = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
    created_on = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
WHERE last_login IS NOT NULL;
Updating the colum with another column values
Syntax:
UPDATE table_name
SET col_name1 = column_name2
WHERE condition;
Eg:
UPDATE account
SET last_login = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
    created_on = last_login
WHERE last_login IS NOT NULL;
Update using join
Syntax:
UPDATE table_name
SET col_name = table_name2.col_name
FROM table_name2
WHERE table_name.col_name = table_name2.col_name;
Eg:
UPDATE account_job
SET hired_on = account.last_login
FROM account
WHERE account_job.user_id = account.user_id;
After the update we can also return the data using below command
UPDATE account
SET last_login = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
    created_on = last_login
RETURNING created_on,last_login, user_id

8. DELETE - Used to delete the row from the table
Syntax:
DELETE FROM table_name
WHERE condition;
RETURNING col_name1, col_name2, ....
Eg:
DELETE FROM job
WHERE job_desc = 'CEO'
RETURNING jobid, job_desc

Below command is used to delete the data from table a based on the same data available in table b
DELETE FROM tabla
USING tableb
WHERE tablea.id = tableb.id
RETURNING tablea.col_name1, tablea.col_name2, ....

